{
  "concept": "As the angle of incidence inside a denser medium increases, the refracted ray bends ever farther from the normal until, at the critical angle where sin(theta_c) = n2/n1, it grazes the interface and beyond which no light escapes — total internal reflection.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Horizontal boundary line splitting the frame; shaded blue rectangle filling the lower half representing the denser medium",
    "Label n1 at right edge inside the shaded lower medium",
    "Label n2 in the unshaded upper region at left",
    "Vertical black dashed normal line through the point of incidence, extending above and below the boundary",
    "Solid teal incident ray traveling up and to the right inside n1, with a directional arrowhead mid-ray, labeled 'Incident ray'",
    "Angle arc and label theta_1 between the incident ray and the normal, below the boundary on the left side",
    "Solid teal reflected ray traveling down and to the right inside n1, arrowhead mid-ray, labeled 'Reflected ray'",
    "Angle arc and label theta_1 between the reflected ray and the normal, below the boundary on the right side",
    "Solid teal refracted ray in n2 bending away from the normal, arrowhead mid-ray, labeled 'Refracted ray'",
    "Angle arc and label theta_2 between the refracted ray and the normal, above the boundary",
    "Grazing-case annotation: small right-angle square at the interface with label 'theta_2 = 90 degrees' when the refracted ray lies along the surface",
    "Dashed teal reference ray drawn at the critical angle theta_c inside n1, with arc and label theta_c",
    "Text annotation 'Total internal reflection' in the lower medium for the supercritical case",
    "Readout panel showing current theta_1, theta_2 (or 'none'), n2/n1, and computed theta_c",
    "Small inset sin-relation caption: n1 sin(theta_1) = n2 sin(theta_2), sin(theta_c) = n2/n1",
    "Panel labels (a), (b), (c) used in the three-case comparison view"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "incidentAngle",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "Increasing theta_1 pushes the refracted ray farther from the normal until it grazes the surface and then vanishes, while the reflected ray persists and brightens.",
      "label": "Angle of incidence θ₁ (degrees)",
      "range": [0, 89, 1],
      "default": 25
    },
    {
      "id": "indexRatio",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "The critical angle is set entirely by the index ratio: sin(theta_c) = n2/n1, so a smaller ratio makes total internal reflection begin at a shallower angle.",
      "label": "Index ratio n₂/n₁",
      "range": [0.3, 0.95, 0.01],
      "default": 0.67
    },
    {
      "id": "viewMode",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "Comparing the live single-boundary view with the textbook's three fixed panels shows that (a), (b), (c) are just three snapshots of one continuously swept angle.",
      "label": "View",
      "options": ["Live single boundary", "Three-panel comparison (a)(b)(c)"],
      "default": "Live single boundary"
    },
    {
      "id": "showCriticalRef",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "Keeping the dashed θ_c reference visible lets the learner judge at a glance whether the current ray is below or above the threshold.",
      "label": "Show dashed θ_c reference ray",
      "default": true
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Ordinary refraction",
      "narration": "At a modest incident angle the ray splits: part reflects back into the dense medium at the same angle θ₁, and part refracts into n₂, bent away from the normal so that θ₂ > θ₁.",
      "state": { "incidentAngle": 25, "indexRatio": 0.67, "viewMode": "Live single boundary", "showCriticalRef": true }
    },
    {
      "title": "Bending farther out",
      "narration": "Push θ₁ higher and the refracted ray swings rapidly toward the surface — θ₂ grows much faster than θ₁ because n₁ sin θ₁ = n₂ sin θ₂ with n₁ > n₂.",
      "state": { "incidentAngle": 36, "indexRatio": 0.67, "viewMode": "Live single boundary", "showCriticalRef": true }
    },
    {
      "title": "The critical angle",
      "narration": "At θ₁ = θ_c the refracted ray lies flat along the boundary, θ₂ = 90°. This is exactly where sin θ_c = n₂/n₁, and the live ray now sits on the dashed reference.",
      "state": { "incidentAngle": 42, "indexRatio": 0.67, "viewMode": "Live single boundary", "showCriticalRef": true }
    },
    {
      "title": "Total internal reflection",
      "narration": "Beyond θ_c there is no solution for θ₂ — the refracted ray disappears entirely and 100% of the light returns into n₁ as the reflected ray.",
      "state": { "incidentAngle": 60, "indexRatio": 0.67, "viewMode": "Live single boundary", "showCriticalRef": true }
    },
    {
      "title": "Moving the threshold",
      "narration": "Hold θ₁ fixed at 60° and raise n₂/n₁: the dashed θ_c line swings outward and, once θ_c exceeds 60°, the refracted ray reappears. The threshold, not the ray, is what the indices control.",
      "state": { "incidentAngle": 60, "indexRatio": 0.92, "viewMode": "Live single boundary", "showCriticalRef": true }
    },
    {
      "title": "Three snapshots of one sweep",
      "narration": "Displayed side by side, panels (a), (b) and (c) are simply θ₁ < θ_c, θ₁ = θ_c and θ₁ > θ_c — one continuous story frozen at three moments.",
      "state": { "incidentAngle": 60, "indexRatio": 0.67, "viewMode": "Three-panel comparison (a)(b)(c)", "showCriticalRef": true }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Total internal reflection is not a new phenomenon but the point where refraction runs out of room: once θ₂ would have to exceed 90°, Snell's law has no solution and all the light must reflect — and that threshold angle is fixed by sin θ_c = n₂/n₁.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Single wide frame (or three equal panels in comparison mode). Lower half is a pale blue-to-white gradient rectangle with a thin blue border, labeled n₁ in serif italic near its right edge; the white region above is labeled n₂ at upper left. A black vertical dashed normal passes through the incidence point at the horizontal center of each panel. All rays are solid teal (#1B7A8C-ish), about 3px wide, each with a filled arrowhead partway along it indicating propagation direction. Incident ray comes from lower-left going up-right; reflected ray leaves to lower-right at a mirrored angle; refracted ray leaves upper-right at a larger angle from the normal. Thin teal or black arcs near the vertex carry italic labels θ₁, θ₁, θ₂, θ_c. At the critical case, draw the refracted ray flat along the boundary to the right with a small right-angle square at the vertex and the label 'θ₂ = 90°' above it. Above θ_c, omit the refracted ray entirely, fatten/brighten the reflected ray, and place the two-line text 'Total internal reflection' in the lower-right of the shaded medium. The dashed teal θ_c reference ray sits in the lower-left quadrant alongside the incident ray. Curved hand-drawn-style annotation arrows connect the words 'Incident ray', 'Reflected ray', 'Refracted ray' to their rays. Keep a small readout box in a corner listing θ₁, θ₂, n₂/n₁ and θ_c with Snell's law written beneath."
}

